<section xml:id="sec-ch01-04-CrossProduct" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>The Cross Product</title>

  <definition>
    <title>The Cross Product Geometric Definition</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given two nonzero vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in
        <m>x y z</m>-space, their cross product, denoted by
        <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>, is a vector orthogonal to both
        <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> with length given by the formula
        <md>
          |\vec{u} \times \vec{v}|=|\vec{u}||\vec{v}| \sin (\theta)
        </md>, where <m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi</m> is the angle between
        <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-cross-product-orthogonal">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.4</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-1.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>Two vectors u and v in xyz-space with their cross product u times v shown as a vector orthogonal to both.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>Two vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in <m>xyz</m>-space with their cross product <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> shown as a vector orthogonal to both.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <title>Direction of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m></title>

    <p>
      Given two nonzero vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> not on the
      same line, to find the direction of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> :
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Put <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in standard position.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Let the fingers of your right hand curl from <m>\vec{u}</m> to
            <m>\vec{v}</m> along <m>\theta</m> (along the angle that is
            between 0 and <m>\pi</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The direction of your thumb as you curl in step 2 is the direction
            of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    We call this method to find the direction of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>
    the right-hand rule.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-right-hand-rule">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.4</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-2.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>An illustration of the right-hand rule: fingers curl from u to v and the thumb points in the direction of u times v.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>An illustration of the right-hand rule: fingers curl from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> and the thumb points in the direction of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-04-ex-4-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine which of the following cross product vectors
        <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> point in the positive <m>z</m>-direction.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-ex-4-1">
        <caption>Four pairs of vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane, cases (a) to (d).</caption>

        <sbsgroup>
          <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
            <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-ex-4-1a">
              <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3.</caption>
              <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-04-ex-4-1a" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
                <instructions>
                  <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
                </instructions>
                <static>
                  <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3.png">
                    <shortdescription>Pair of vectors u and v in the xy-plane for case a. The angle from u to v is 3 pi over 4.</shortdescription>
                    <description>
                      <p>Pair of vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane for case a. The angle from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{3\pi}{4}</m>.</p>
                    </description>
                  </image>
                </static>
              </interactive>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-ex-4-1b">
              <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3-2.</caption>
              <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-04-ex-4-1b" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
                <instructions>
                  <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
                </instructions>
                <static>
                  <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3-2.png">
                    <shortdescription>Pair of vectors u and v in the xy-plane for case b. The angle from u to v is 13 pi over 12.</shortdescription>
                    <description>
                      <p>Pair of vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane for case b. The angle from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{13\pi}{12}</m>.</p>
                    </description>
                  </image>
                </static>
              </interactive>
            </figure>
          </sidebyside>

          <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="auto" valign="middle">
            <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-ex-4-1c">
              <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3-3.</caption>
              <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-04-ex-4-1c" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
                <instructions>
                  <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
                </instructions>
                <static>
                  <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3-3.png">
                    <shortdescription>Pair of vectors u and v in the xy-plane for case c. The angle from u to v is 5 pi over 4.</shortdescription>
                    <description>
                      <p>Pair of vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane for case c. The angle from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m>.</p>
                    </description>
                  </image>
                </static>
              </interactive>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-ex-4-1d">
              <caption>This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3-1.</caption>
              <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-04-ex-4-1d" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
                <instructions>
                  <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here: <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
                </instructions>
                <static>
                  <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-3-1.png">
                    <shortdescription>Pair of vectors u and v in the xy-plane for case d. The angle from u to v is 5 pi over 12.</shortdescription>
                    <description>
                      <p>Pair of vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane for case d. The angle from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{5\pi}{12}</m>.</p>
                    </description>
                  </image>
                </static>
              </interactive>
            </figure>
          </sidebyside>
        </sbsgroup>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        In each of the figures (a)<ndash/>(d) above, both vectors lie in the
        <m>xy</m>-plane, so <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> points either in the
        positive or in the negative <m>z</m>-direction. By the right-hand
        rule, it points in the positive <m>z</m>-direction exactly when curling
        from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> along the angle <m>\theta</m>
        between them (<m>0 \leq \theta \leq \pi</m>) is a counterclockwise
        rotation.
      </p>

      <p>
        In (a) the counterclockwise rotation from <m>\vec{u}</m> to
        <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{3\pi}{4} \lt \pi</m>, so this rotation is
        along the angle between the vectors, and <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>
        points in the positive <m>z</m>-direction. The same happens in (d),
        where the counterclockwise rotation is <m>\frac{5\pi}{12} \lt \pi</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        In (b) the counterclockwise rotation from <m>\vec{u}</m> to
        <m>\vec{v}</m> is <m>\frac{13\pi}{12} \gt \pi</m>, so the angle between
        the vectors is <m>2\pi-\frac{13\pi}{12} = \frac{11\pi}{12}</m>, and
        curling from <m>\vec{u}</m> to <m>\vec{v}</m> along this angle is a
        clockwise rotation: <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> points in the
        negative <m>z</m>-direction. The same happens in (c), where the angle
        between the vectors is <m>2\pi-\frac{5\pi}{4} = \frac{3\pi}{4}</m>,
        again traversed clockwise.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence only the cross products in (a) and (d) point in the positive
        <m>z</m>-direction.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}=\vec{0}</m> (if <m>\theta=0</m> or
            <m>\theta=\pi</m> ), the direction of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>
            is undefined.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            For nonzero <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>, the cross product
            is not commutative. In fact, <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}=</m>
            <m>-(\vec{v} \times \vec{u})</m> (they have the same length, but
            point in opposite directions).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-04-ex-4-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the figure below, find:
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The direction of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The magnitude of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The vector <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-ex-4-2">
        <caption>Vector <m>\vec{u}=\langle 0,2,0\rangle</m>, and vector <m>\vec{v}=\langle 0,0,4\rangle</m>, in <m>xyz</m>-space.</caption>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch01-04-ex-4-2" variant="controls" width="90%" aspect="3:2"
          calcplot3d="type=vector;vector=%3C0,%202,%200%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(255,193,7);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C0,%200,%204%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(30,136,229);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=undefined&amp;type=text;text=v~%3C0%2C0%2C4%3E;visible=true;point=(0,0.2,4);color=rgb(30,136,229);font=Times%20New%20Roman;fontsize=14pt;bold=true;italic=false;fontmath=true;align=Lower-right&amp;type=text;text=u~%3C0%2C2%2C0%3E;visible=true;point=(0,2.5,0);color=rgb(255,193,7);font=Times%20New%20Roman;fontsize=14pt;bold=true;italic=false;fontmath=true;align=Upper-right&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=8.236391035463319,4.755282581475766,3.0901699437494745,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0,0,2,1;transparent=false;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=true;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=true;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-4;xmax=4;ymin=-4;ymax=4;zmin=-4;zmax=4;xscale=2;yscale=2;zscale=2;zcmin=-8;zcmax=8;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=0.51">
          <instructions>
            <p>
              Drag with the mouse to rotate the surface. You can open the plot in a new page from here: <url href="http://c3d.libretexts.org/CalcPlot3D/index.html?type=vector;vector=%3C0,%202,%200%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(255,193,7);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=false&amp;type=vector;vector=%3C0,%200,%204%3E;visible=true;color=rgb(30,136,229);size=2;initialpt=(0,0,0);dashed=undefined&amp;type=text;text=v~%3C0%2C0%2C4%3E;visible=true;point=(0,0.2,4);color=rgb(30,136,229);font=Times%20New%20Roman;fontsize=14pt;bold=true;italic=false;fontmath=true;align=Lower-right&amp;type=text;text=u~%3C0%2C2%2C0%3E;visible=true;point=(0,2.5,0);color=rgb(255,193,7);font=Times%20New%20Roman;fontsize=14pt;bold=true;italic=false;fontmath=true;align=Upper-right&amp;type=window;showfunnot=false;hsrmode=0;nomidpts=true;anaglyph=-1;center=8.236391035463319,4.755282581475766,3.0901699437494745,1;focus=0,0,0,1;up=0,0,2,1;transparent=false;alpha=140;twoviews=false;unlinkviews=false;axisextension=0.7;shownormals=false;shownormalsatpts=false;xaxislabel=x;yaxislabel=y;zaxislabel=z;edgeson=true;faceson=true;showbox=true;showaxes=true;showticks=true;perspective=true;centerxpercent=0.5;centerypercent=0.5;rotationsteps=30;autospin=true;xygrid=false;yzgrid=false;xzgrid=false;gridsonbox=true;gridplanes=false;gridcolor=rgb(128,128,128);lastaddedsurfaceactive=true;disabletrace=false;activefun=-1;xmin=-4;xmax=4;ymin=-4;ymax=4;zmin=-4;zmax=4;xscale=2;yscale=2;zscale=2;zcmin=-8;zcmax=8;xscalefactor=1;yscalefactor=1;zscalefactor=1;tracemode=0;keep2d=false;zoom=0.51" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the plot</url>
            </p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-4.png">
              <shortdescription>Vector u with coordinates x = 0, y = 2, z = 0, and vector v with coordinates x = 0, y = 0, z = 4, in xyz-space.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>Vector <m>\vec{u}=\langle 0,2,0\rangle</m>, and vector <m>\vec{v}=\langle 0,0,4\rangle</m>, in <m>xyz</m>-space.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From the figure, <m>\vec{u} = \langle 0,2,0 \rangle</m> lies on the
        positive <m>y</m>-axis and <m>\vec{v} = \langle 0,0,4 \rangle</m> lies
        on the positive <m>z</m>-axis. Curling the fingers of the right hand
        from the positive <m>y</m>-axis towards the positive <m>z</m>-axis,
        the thumb points in the positive <m>x</m>-direction. Hence
        <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> points in the positive
        <m>x</m>-direction (the direction of <m>\vec{i}</m>).
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The angle between the positive <m>y</m>-axis and the positive
        <m>z</m>-axis is <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>, so
        <md>
          |\vec{u} \times \vec{v}| = |\vec{u}||\vec{v}|\sin\left( \frac{\pi}{2} \right) = 2 \cdot 4 \cdot 1 = 8
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Combining the two previous parts, <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> is
        the vector of length 8 pointing in the positive <m>x</m>-direction:
        <md>
          \vec{u} \times \vec{v} = 8\vec{i} = \langle 8,0,0 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <definition>
    <title>Cross Products Computational Definition</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given two vectors
        <m>\vec{u}=\left\langle u_{1}, u_{2}, u_{3}\right\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\left\langle v_{1}, v_{2}, v_{3}\right\rangle</m> (they
        could be the zero vector) in <m>x y z</m>-space,
        <md>
          <mrow>\vec{u} \times \vec{v} \amp =\left|\begin{array}{ccc} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ u_{1} \amp u_{2} \amp u_{3} \\ v_{1} \amp v_{2} \amp v_{3} \end{array}\right|=\left|\begin{array}{ll} u_{2} \amp u_{3} \\ v_{2} \amp v_{3} \end{array}\right| \vec{i}-\left|\begin{array}{ll} u_{1} \amp u_{3} \\ v_{1} \amp v_{3} \end{array}\right| \vec{j}+\left|\begin{array}{ll} u_{1} \amp u_{2} \\ v_{1} \amp v_{2} \end{array}\right| \vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp =\left(u_{2} v_{3}-u_{3} v_{2}\right) \vec{i}-\left(u_{1} v_{3}-u_{3} v_{1}\right) \vec{j}+\left(u_{1} v_{2}-u_{2} v_{1}\right) \vec{k}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-04-ex-4-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m> given that
        <m>\vec{u}=\langle 1,2,3\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 2,1,3\rangle</m>. What is
        <m>\vec{v} \times \vec{u}</m> ?
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using the computational definition:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{u} \times \vec{v} = \left| \begin{matrix} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \end{matrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left| \begin{matrix} 2 \amp 3 \\ 1 \amp 3 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{i} - \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 3 \\ 2 \amp 3 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{j} + \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp 1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left( 2(3)-3(1) \right)\vec{i} - \left( 1(3)-3(2) \right)\vec{j} + \left( 1(1)-2(2) \right)\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= 3\vec{i}+3\vec{j}-3\vec{k} = \langle 3,3,-3 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        For the second question there is no need to compute another
        determinant: since
        <m>\vec{v} \times \vec{u} = -(\vec{u} \times \vec{v})</m>,
        <md>
          \vec{v} \times \vec{u} = -\langle 3,3,-3 \rangle = \langle -3,-3,3 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>The Cross Product and Parallel Vectors</title>

    <p>
      Given two vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>, if
      <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}=\vec{0}, \vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> are
      parallel (lie on the same line).
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-04-ex-4-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether the vectors <m>\vec{u}=\langle -3,6,0\rangle</m>
        and <m>\vec{v}=\langle1,-2,0\rangle</m> lie on the same line.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        We compute the cross product:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{u} \times \vec{v} = \left| \begin{matrix} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ -3 \amp 6 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp -2 \amp 0 \end{matrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left| \begin{matrix} 6 \amp 0 \\ -2 \amp 0 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{i} - \left| \begin{matrix} -3 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{j} + \left| \begin{matrix} -3 \amp 6 \\ 1 \amp -2 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left( 6(0)-0(-2) \right)\vec{i} - \left( (-3)(0)-0(1) \right)\vec{j} + \left( (-3)(-2)-6(1) \right)\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= 0\vec{i}-0\vec{j}+(6-6)\vec{k} = \vec{0}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Since <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>, the vectors are
        parallel, and hence they lie on the same line.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Another way to determine whether two vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and
      <m>\vec{v}</m> are parallel is to check whether there exists a scalar
      <m>c</m> such that <m>c \vec{u}=\vec{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-04-ex-4-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Determine whether <m>\vec{u}=\langle 1,2,1\rangle</m> and
        <m>\vec{v}=\langle 2,-1,2\rangle</m> are parallel.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Suppose there were a scalar <m>c</m> such that
        <m>c\vec{u} = \vec{v}</m>. Comparing components:
        <md>
          c\cdot 1 = 2 \Rightarrow c = 2, \quad c \cdot  2 = -1 \Rightarrow c = -\frac{1}{2}, \quad c \cdot 1 = 2 \Rightarrow c = 2
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        The second component requires a different scalar than the other two,
        so no such <m>c</m> exists and the vectors are not parallel. We can
        confirm this with the cross product:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{u} \times \vec{v} = \left| \begin{matrix} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp -1 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left| \begin{matrix} 2 \amp 1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{i} - \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 2 \amp 2 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{j} + \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 2 \\ 2 \amp -1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left( 2(2)-1(-1) \right)\vec{i} - \left( 1(2)-1(2) \right)\vec{j} + \left( 1(-1)-2(2) \right)\vec{k} = \langle 5,0,-5 \rangle \neq \vec{0}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>The Cross Product and Parallelograms</title>

    <p>
      Given two nonzero vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and
      <m>\vec{v},|\vec{u} \times \vec{v}|</m> is the area of the
      parallelogram defined by <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch01-04-parallelogram">
    <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, Section 2.4</caption>

    <image source="/images/2025_08_04_6cd67e91fafb048556b2g-6.jpg" width="50%">
      <shortdescription>A parallelogram defined by vectors u and v, with the area equal to the magnitude of u cross product v.</shortdescription>
      <description>
        <p>A parallelogram defined by vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m>, with the area equal to the magnitude of <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}</m>.</p>
      </description>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <example xml:id="example-ch01-04-ex-4-6">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the area of the triangle with vertices given by the points
        <m>A(1,2,3), B(2,3,4), C(4,3,2)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The triangle is half of the parallelogram defined by the vectors
        <m>\vec{AB}</m> and <m>\vec{AC}</m>, so its area is
        <m>\frac{1}{2}\left| \vec{AB} \times \vec{AC} \right|</m>. In standard
        position:
        <md>
          \vec{AB} = \langle 2-1, 3-2, 4-3 \rangle = \langle 1,1,1 \rangle, \quad \vec{AC} = \langle 4-1, 3-2, 2-3 \rangle = \langle 3,1,-1 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Their cross product is
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{AB} \times \vec{AC} = \left| \begin{matrix} \vec{i} \amp \vec{j} \amp \vec{k} \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp 1 \amp -1 \end{matrix}\right|</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp -1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{i} - \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp -1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{j} + \left| \begin{matrix} 1 \amp 1 \\ 3 \amp 1 \end{matrix}\right|\vec{k}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \left( 1(-1)-1(1) \right)\vec{i} - \left( 1(-1)-1(3) \right)\vec{j} + \left( 1(1)-1(3) \right)\vec{k} = \langle -2,4,-2 \rangle</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        with magnitude
        <md>
          \left| \vec{AB} \times \vec{AC} \right| = \sqrt{(-2)^2+4^2+(-2)^2} = \sqrt{24} = 2\sqrt{6}
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence the area of the triangle is
        <m>\frac{1}{2} \cdot 2\sqrt{6} = \sqrt{6}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Properties of the Cross Product
    </p>

    <p>
      Given two vectors <m>\vec{u}</m> and <m>\vec{v}</m> in
      <m>x y z</m>-space and <m>a, b</m> real numbers, then
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{u} \times \vec{v}=-(\vec{v} \times \vec{u})</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>(a \vec{u}) \times(b \vec{v})=a b(\vec{u} \times \vec{v})</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\vec{u} \times(\vec{v}+\vec{w})=(\vec{u} \times \vec{v})+(\vec{u} \times \vec{w})</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>(\vec{u}+\vec{v}) \times \vec{w}=(\vec{u} \times \vec{w})+(\vec{v} \times \vec{w})</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

</section>
